So can we find this ratio in terms of

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.200
‘n one’ and ‘n two’?

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:06.820
Yes we can!

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.260
We just use some very basic math!

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.000
We can write this ratio like this.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:15.360
Where ‘c’ is the speed of light in vacuum!

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:19.080
Yes, if you simplify this ratio, we will get

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:20.560
‘v one' over 'v two’.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:22.720
And can you guess what the numerator and

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.780
the denominator are equal to?

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.780
That's right, this is nothing but the absolute refractive

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.500
index of medium 2 and this is the absolute

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.000
refractive index of medium 1.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:38.020
That was another way of understanding the Snell’s law!

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.520
We can also say that ‘sine i' over 'sine r’

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.180
is ‘n two' over 'n one’.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.140
Where ‘n two’ and ‘n one’ are the

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:49.620
absolute refractive indices of media 2 and 1 respectively!

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.280
Hope you understood this well!

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.880
Let’s move on to a new page!

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.660
So these were the two laws of refraction of light.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.300
In the next lesson, we will solve a simple

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.720
example based on Snell’s law!

